I don't think adding an explicit hint is the right solution here. Any `Variant::INT` without an explicit range should behave with the default settings:
```cpp
struct EditorPropertyRangeHint {
	bool or_greater = true;
	bool or_less = true;
	double min = -99999.0;
	double max = 99999.0;
	double step = 1.0;
	String suffix;
	bool exp_range = false;
	bool hide_slider = true;
	bool radians = false;
};

...

static EditorPropertyRangeHint _parse_range_hint(PropertyHint p_hint, const String &p_hint_text, double p_default_step) { ... }

...

		case Variant::INT: {
				...

				EditorPropertyInteger *editor = memnew(EditorPropertyInteger);

				EditorPropertyRangeHint hint = _parse_range_hint(p_hint, p_hint_text, 1);
				editor->setup(hint.min, hint.max, hint.step, hint.hide_slider, hint.or_greater, hint.or_less, hint.suffix);

				return editor;
```

The code reads like this is the intent. So something is broken.

It's a regression from #69797, in `3.x` `EditorPropertyInteger::setup` doesn't set `hide_slider` on the spinbox. CC @TokageItLab 

```diff
diff --git a/editor/editor_properties.cpp b/editor/editor_properties.cpp
index 33bba90c70..18898a74f3 100644
--- a/editor/editor_properties.cpp
+++ b/editor/editor_properties.cpp
@@ -4170,7 +4170,7 @@ struct EditorPropertyRangeHint {
        double step = 1.0;
        String suffix;
        bool exp_range = false;
-       bool hide_slider = true;
+       bool hide_slider = false;
        bool radians = false;
 };
 ```
will likely solve it (only hide the slider if it's passed in the range hint), but I need to test what impact it will have on floats.
